WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 51: The Villisca Axe Murders: The Unsolved Midnight Mystery of 1912

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.680
[Rebecca] Welcome back to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:06.960
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:19.840
[Rebecca] In the quiet, pre-dawn hours of June 10, 1912, the peaceful farming community of Villiska, Iowa, was shattered by one of the most gruesome, baffling, and infamous unsolved crimes in American history.

4
00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:27.120
[Rebecca] An entire family of six, along with two young neighbor girls who had stayed for a sleepover, were murdered in their beds with an axe.

5
00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:37.120
[Rebecca] The killer left no fingerprints, stole no money, systematically draped every mirror and window in cloth, and vanished into the summer darkness like a ghost.

6
00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:45.600
[Rebecca] George brings us the chilling history of the Villisca tragedy and the century of questions that followed.

7
00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:54.480
[George] On Sunday, June 9, 1912, the quiet, prosperous town of Villiska, Iowa, was bathed in early summer warmth.

8
00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:55.520
[George] Josiah B.

9
00:00:55.520 --> 00:01:04.080
[George] Moore, a respected and prominent 43-year-old farm implement merchant, attended Children's Day services at the local Presbyterian church.

10
00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:15.920
[George] With him were his devoted wife Sarah and their four young children, Herman, aged 11, Catherine, aged 10, Boyd, aged seven, and young Paul, aged five.

11
00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:30.000
[George] Accompanying the Moore family were two neighboring sisters, Innae and Lena Stillinger, aged eight and twelve, who were close friends of the Moore children and had received enthusiastic permission from their parents to spend the night at the Moore home.

12
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:37.920
[George] The evening service was a joyful celebration filled with musical recitations, floral arrangements, and children singing hymns.

13
00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:42.080
[George] Shortly after 9:30 in the evening, the program concluded.

14
00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:52.640
[George] Josiah, Sarah, the children, and the Stillinger girls walked the three blocks back to the Moore residence on East 2nd Street, a modest two-story wooden frame house.

15
00:01:52.640 --> 00:02:03.560
[George] They locked the screen doors, settled in for the night, and extinguished the kerosene lamps, completely unaware that a cold-blooded intruder was already hiding in the upstairs shadows.

16
00:02:03.560 --> 00:02:13.800
[George] The front yard of the Moor home was bordered by neat rosebushes and a picket fence, reflecting the modest, hardworking character of a prosperous Midwestern implement merchant.

17
00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:18.680
[George] The following morning, Monday, June 10, dawned bright and clear.

18
00:02:18.680 --> 00:02:25.720
[George] At approximately 7:30, next-door neighbor Mary Peckham stepped out into her yard to hang washing on the clothesline.

19
00:02:25.720 --> 00:02:31.240
[George] She immediately noticed an unsettling, unnatural stillness hanging over the Moor property.

20
00:02:31.240 --> 00:02:41.000
[George] The livestock in the barn had not been fed or turned out to pasture, the chickens were still locked in their coops squawking for grain, and the morning chores had not begun.

21
00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:47.080
[George] Mary walked across the lawn and knocked firmly on the back screen door, but received no answer.

22
00:02:47.080 --> 00:02:51.560
[George] She tried the door handle, finding it securely latched from the inside.

23
00:02:51.560 --> 00:02:59.160
[George] Peering through the lace curtains of the kitchen window, everything appeared undisturbed, but the oppressive silence felt wrong.

24
00:02:59.160 --> 00:03:06.200
[George] Growing increasingly alarmed, Mary telephoned Josiah's brother, Ross Moore, at his downtown store.

25
00:03:06.200 --> 00:03:09.960
[George] Ross arrived within twenty minutes with a spare ring of keys.

26
00:03:09.960 --> 00:03:16.280
[George] He knocked loudly on the front porch and called out the family names, but was answered only by silence.

27
00:03:16.280 --> 00:03:19.880
[George] Ross unlocked the front door and pushed it open into the parlor.

28
00:03:19.880 --> 00:03:24.520
[George] The interior was pitch black, as window shades had been drawn tight.

29
00:03:24.520 --> 00:03:31.400
[George] Striking a sulfur match in the darkness, Ross was hit by the heavy, nauseating stench of fresh copper and blood.

30
00:03:31.400 --> 00:03:37.160
[George] Stepping toward the downstairs guest bedroom, he saw dark stains soaked into the bedcovers.

31
00:03:37.160 --> 00:03:44.200
[George] Terrified, Ross ran out onto the lawn and shouted to Mary Peckham to summon Town Marshal Henry Horton immediately.

32
00:03:44.200 --> 00:03:54.240
[George] The morning breeze carried the sweet scent of clover across the porch, making the grim discovery inside the darkened parlor even more shocking to the quiet farm community.

33
00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:57.200
[George] When Marshal Henry Horton and local physician Dr.

34
00:03:57.200 --> 00:03:57.440
[George] J.

35
00:03:57.440 --> 00:04:02.640
[George] Clark Cooper entered the residence, they encountered a nightmare beyond comprehension.

36
00:04:02.640 --> 00:04:09.040
[George] In the downstairs bedroom lay the lifeless bodies of eight-year-old Inne and twelve-year-old Lena Stillinger.

37
00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:14.400
[George] Upstairs in the master bedroom, Josiah and Sarah Moore had been murdered in their bed.

38
00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:20.400
[George] Down the hallway in the second upstairs room, all four more children lay bludgeoned to death.

39
00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:30.800
[George] The murder weapon was Josiah's own heavy two-pound felling axe, which was found leaning against the wall in the downstairs guest room, its blade and pole stained with blood.

40
00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:36.640
[George] But the bizarre psychological signatures left behind by the killer chilled seasoned lawman to the core.

41
00:04:36.640 --> 00:04:48.880
[George] The murderer had systematically draped every mirror and window in the house with shirts, dresses, aprons and bed sheets, as if desperate to avoid seeing his reflection or being observed from the outside.

42
00:04:48.880 --> 00:04:52.640
[George] The faces of all eight victims were covered with bed linens.

43
00:04:52.640 --> 00:05:00.480
[George] A four-pound slab of cured slab bacon, wrapped carefully in a towel, was discovered lying on the floor near the axe.

44
00:05:00.480 --> 00:05:13.040
[George] On the kitchen table, a basin of dark, bloody water sat beside a half eaten plate of food, proving that the killer had calmly washed his hands and lingeringly eaten in the kitchen before slipping out into the dawn.

45
00:05:13.040 --> 00:05:22.720
[George] No drawers were ransacked and Josiah Gold Pocket Watch remained untouched on the dresser, proving to investigators that robbery was never the motive for the slaughter.

46
00:05:22.720 --> 00:05:28.320
[George] Within hours, word of the slaughter spread, and Villisca descended into mass panic.

47
00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:38.920
[George] An estimated 200 curious townsfolk and farmers trampled through the house before law enforcement could establish a perimeter, destroying vital footprints and forensic evidence.

48
00:05:38.920 --> 00:05:49.000
[George] Bloodhounds brought by train from Beatrice, Nebraska, caught a faint scent leading toward the Burlington Railroad tracks, but the trail dissolved in the gravel ballast.

49
00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:54.440
[George] The community demanded a culprit, and suspicion fell heavily upon Reverend George Kelly.

50
00:05:54.440 --> 00:06:07.160
[George] Kelly was an eccentric, diminutive traveling Presbyterian minister from England who had attended the Children's Day service on Sunday evening and hastily departed Villiska on the five o'clock train Monday morning.

51
00:06:07.160 --> 00:06:13.960
[George] Kelly had a documented history of severe mental instability, religious hallucinations, and voyeurism.

52
00:06:13.960 --> 00:06:21.960
[George] In the weeks following the murders, Kelly sent rambling, obsessed letters to investigators discussing the crimes in intimate detail.

53
00:06:21.960 --> 00:06:32.600
[George] In 1917, during an intense interrogation by state agents, Kelly signed a confession stating that the voice of God had commanded him to slay utterly with the axe.

54
00:06:32.600 --> 00:06:41.240
[George] However, at his trial, Kelly recanted the confession, demonstrating that it had been extracted under extreme duress and sleep deprivation.

55
00:06:41.240 --> 00:06:47.480
[George] The jury deliberated for days, deadlocked, and a second trial resulted in Kelly's full acquittal.

56
00:06:47.480 --> 00:06:59.720
[George] Church records and local diaries described Kelly as a bizarre eccentric who frequently muttered incoherently during services, claiming that divine visions compelled him to punish unrighteous communities.

57
00:06:59.720 --> 00:07:08.440
[George] A second, explosive theory emerged through the efforts of private investigator James Newton Wilkerson of the famous Burns Detective Agency.

58
00:07:08.440 --> 00:07:19.200
[George] Wilkerson was hired by local citizens and quickly pointed the finger at prominent Iowa State Senator Frank Fernando Jones, a wealthy Villisca banker and lumberyard owner.

59
00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:30.880
[George] Josiah Moore had previously worked for Jones for nine years as a manager before breaking away to open his own competing implement business, taking the lucrative John Deere dealership contract with him.

60
00:07:30.880 --> 00:07:42.320
[George] Wilkerson alleged that Senator Jones harbored intense personal hatred toward Moore and had hired a professional tranchant killer named William Blackie Mansfield to assassinate the Moore family.

61
00:07:42.320 --> 00:07:50.560
[George] Mansfield was a vagrant and slaughterhouse worker who had previously been investigated for axe murders of his own wife and child in Kansas.

62
00:07:50.560 --> 00:07:57.120
[George] Wilkerson mounted a fiery public crusade, delivering impassioned speeches in the local opera house.

63
00:07:57.120 --> 00:08:05.200
[George] However, grand juries investigated the charges and found Mansfield had an airtight payroll alibi in Illinois on the night of the murders.

64
00:08:05.200 --> 00:08:10.400
[George] Senator Jones sued Wilkerson for criminal slander, winning a massive judgment.

65
00:08:10.400 --> 00:08:18.000
[George] The bitter controversy fractured the town of Villisca into hostile camps of neighbors who refused to speak to one another for fifty years.

66
00:08:18.000 --> 00:08:29.200
[George] The fierce animosity between the Jones and Moore factions polarized church congregations and business partnerships, turning former friends into lifelong enemies across Montgomery County.

67
00:08:29.200 --> 00:08:42.160
[George] In recent decades, modern criminologists, historians, and FBI behavioral analysts have re-examined the cold case files, looking beyond small-town grudges to uncover a chilling broader pattern.

68
00:08:42.160 --> 00:08:50.400
[George] Between 1911 and 1912, an identical string of axe murders occurred in small railroad communities across the Midwest.

69
00:08:50.400 --> 00:08:59.200
[George] Colorado Springs, Colorado, Monmouth, Illinois, Ellsworth, Kansas, and Paula, Kansas, in every single incident.

70
00:08:59.200 --> 00:09:04.680
[George] An entire sleeping family was bludgeoned with the blunt side of an axe found on the property.

71
00:09:04.680 --> 00:09:18.040
[George] Mirrors and windows were carefully covered with cloth, kerosene lamp chimneys were removed, the killer washed in a kitchen basin, and the perpetrator vanished along the railroad tracks without stealing money or jewelry.

72
00:09:18.040 --> 00:09:31.480
[George] Modern criminal profiling strongly links these crimes to Paul Mueller, a German immigrant and tranchant farm laborer who had been the prime suspect in an identical 1897 axe murder of a family in Massachusetts.

73
00:09:31.480 --> 00:09:42.200
[George] Mueller was known to ride freight trains across the American heartland, working seasonal agricultural jobs and leaving a trail of nocturnal slaughter along the iron rails.

74
00:09:42.200 --> 00:09:45.640
[George] No one was ever convicted of the Villisca Axe murders.

75
00:09:45.640 --> 00:09:58.200
[George] The tragedy permanently shattered the naive frontier assumption that small town rural America was immune to horrific violence, and across Iowa, families began locking their doors for the first time in history.

76
00:09:58.200 --> 00:10:04.680
[George] Today, the Josiah Moore home still stands at 508 East 2nd Street in Villisca.

77
00:10:04.680 --> 00:10:19.320
[George] Carefully restored to its exact 1912 condition, preserved without running water or modern electrical wiring, it serves as a historic museum and site of pilgrimage for historians, criminologists, and paranormal investigators.

78
00:10:19.320 --> 00:10:34.840
[George] More than a century after that tragic summer night, the quiet white frame house remains a solemn and haunting monument to eight innocent lives cut short, and an enduring reminder of one of the deepest, most chilling unsolved mysteries in American history.

79
00:10:35.480 --> 00:10:41.960
[Rebecca] The Villisca Axe Murders remain one of history's deepest enigmas, etched into the American conscience.

80
00:10:41.960 --> 00:10:43.320
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

81
00:10:43.320 --> 00:10:49.840
[Rebecca] Join us next week as we journey north into the pine forests of Minnesota for the astonishing rescue train of Jimmy.

